\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Cylinder weights extend to a Borel probability}
\date{2026-11-07}

\p{Let #{\mu_1,\mu_2,\mu_3} be positive reals summing to #{1},
and let #{\Sigma} be the infinite words on #{\{1,2,3\}}.
Write #{C_w} for the words that begin with a finite word
#{w}. There is a Borel probability #{\nu} on #{\Sigma} with
##{\nu(C_w)=\mu_{w_1}\cdots\mu_{w_n}}
whenever #{w} has length #{n}. The running choice is
#{\mu_i=\frac{1}{3}}.}

\proof{
\p{The cylinder #{C_w} is the disjoint union of its three
one-letter extensions, and those weights sum to the weight
of #{C_w}. Let #{A} be the finite unions of cylinders.
Refinement to a common length shows that a finite disjoint
decomposition has a mass independent of the chosen
cylinders, so the formula defines a finitely additive
probability on the algebra #{A}. Each set in #{A} is
compact, being a finite union of cylinders. A countable
cover of a set in #{A} by sets in #{A} therefore has a
finite subcover, and finite additivity gives countable
additivity on #{A}.}

\p{For an arbitrary set #{E} define #{\nu_*(E)} to be the
infimum of #{\sum\nu(B_j)} over covers of #{E} by sets
#{B_j\in A}. This set function vanishes on the empty set,
is monotone, and is countably subadditive. Compactness
gives #{\nu_*(B)=\nu(B)} for #{B\in A}. Call #{E}
measurable when
#{\nu_*(T)\ge\nu_*(T\cap E)+\nu_*(T\cap E^{c})}
for every test set #{T}. Subadditivity supplies the reverse
inequality. If #{E\in A} and #{T} is covered by sets of
#{A} up to a small tolerance, those sets split along #{E}
into members of #{A}, so #{E} is measurable.}

\p{The inequality is symmetric in a set and its complement.
Let #{E} and #{F} be measurable. Apply it to #{E}, then to
#{F} on each of the two pieces:
##{\nu_*(T)\ge\nu_*(T\cap E\cap F)+\nu_*(T\cap E\cap F^{c})+\nu_*(T\cap E^{c}\cap F)+\nu_*(T\cap E^{c}\cap F^{c}).}
The first three sets cover #{T\cap(E\cup F)}. Subadditivity
therefore yields the same inequality for #{E\cup F}, so a
finite union of measurable sets is measurable. Complements
give finite intersections. For a countable union,
successive differences produce a disjoint decomposition to
which the finite case applies. At stage #{n} the remainder
is at least the complement of the whole union, and
countable subadditivity on that union closes the
inequality. The union is measurable. The test set equal to
the union, together with subadditivity, gives
#{\nu_*(E)=\sum_i\nu_*(E_i)}. The measurable sets form a
#{\sigma}-algebra containing #{A}. Every open set is a
countable union of cylinders, hence measurable, and so is
every Borel set. Restricting #{\nu_*} gives the Borel
probability.}
}
